  "summary": "U.S. rates led the session. Two-year through ten-year yields rose 12 to 14 basis points, the ten-year is back above 5 at 5.1, and those yields sit in the top decile of the past year's levels. SOFR futures still price policy well above cash SOFR of 3.89, after October consumer prices rose 0.4 percent and Fed commentary turned toward another inflation fight. The dollar firmed with that move. Dollar-Canada and dollar-Swiss are near the top of their one-year ranges, while the euro, sterling, the Australian dollar, and the New Zealand dollar weakened. Canada's yields rose with Treasuries. Australia's cash rate and forward path are flat to easier, not the same further-hike path. Euro-area and Japanese long yields barely moved. Brent is 95.82 and WTI is 88.74. Equities are still high and the VIX is 17.5, so this is a rates and dollar story rather than an equity break. Today's U.S. producer prices and the euro-area GDP flash are the next test, with Canada CPI and Japan GDP on Monday.",
